The morning rush is over.
It is finally quiet in the café.
Elif and I lean against the counter at the back.
We drink a coffee together.
"You really did well today," Elif praises.
"My first coffee went totally wrong," I say and laugh.
"But my second coffee turned out much better."
"That is totally normal," she says.
"You simply need time to learn to make a good espresso."
I nod, because the machine is really complicated.
But I have already learned a lot today.
We chat relaxedly about the neighbourhood.
Elif tells me about the association.
"We have planned many events for the summer," she says.
"Next month we are organizing a flea market."
"That sounds great," I answer.
"Maybe I can help there too?"
"Very gladly!"
"We are always looking for volunteers," Elif says and smiles.
A new guest comes through the door.
"My third coffee will be perfect," I say and go to the machine.
